# Performance quality standards

- Establish a baseline before optimization: target hardware assumptions, scene, entity count, frame time, memory, and reproduction steps.
- Profile the actual bottleneck before choosing an optimization.
- Distinguish CPU, GPU, memory, I/O, physics, animation, and draw-call costs.
- Consider worst-case combat density, not only an empty test scene.
- Prefer existing pooling, batching, spatial partitioning, LOD, culling, and data-oriented patterns where available.
- Do not introduce pooling or caching without defining ownership, reset behavior, capacity, and overflow behavior.
- Verify visual and gameplay correctness after optimization.
- Report measured before/after results or explicitly state when measurement was unavailable.
